{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1M3H5cJY6A7FgYdvYLM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. I did not change `/work/combi_imo_2011_p4.lean`, so it still contains the original `sorry`.\n\nI checked the statement on small cases: n=1 gives 1 and n=2 gives 3, matching (2n-1)!!. So the theorem looks true, but I could not produce a complete Lean proof for general n.\n\nThe mathematical argument I worked out is this. Let G(m, j) count placements of m weights where only the first j steps are checked. The heaviest weight 2^(m-1) must sit on the left whenever it is checked, because it outweighs all lighter weights combined. Splitting on its placement time p gives G(m, j) = Σ_{p≤j} G(m-1, p-1) + 2(m-j)·G(m-1, j), with G(0, 0) = 1. Then G(n, n) = Σ_{q<n} G(n-1, q) reproduces (2n-1)!!.\n\nThe obstacle is formalization. Turning this into Lean means reindexing permutations of `Fin n` when the heaviest weight is removed, and building bijections between the filtered `Finset` of pairs `(σ, f)` and the recursive sequences. I could not carry that out reliably with the tools available here."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":932,"cache_read_input_tokens":1885,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":932,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a429fc23-06ff-4f1b-8188-58a7662f2e14","uuid":"bcf2d172-a7ad-46be-8740-cdfbe4c32234","timestamp":"2026-10-14T02:33:08.633Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1M3Gd5xJtKBNdrEvqPT"}
